\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}

Draws an angular column of an aniframe as a circular histogram, or rose
diagram: one wedge per bin of directions, its size the share of the group's
angles that fall in the bin. Use it for any angle the frame stores in its
angular unit, such as \code{course} from \code{animetric::calculate_kinematics()} or a
2D orientation (\code{heading}) declared with \code{animetric::add_orientation()}.

The plot takes its conventions from the frame rather than assuming them:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Unit:} the column is read in the frame's \code{unit_angle}
(\code{\link[anicore:get_metadata]{anicore::get_metadata()}}) and binned in radians
(\code{\link[anicore:angle_to_rad]{anicore::angle_to_rad()}}). The angular axis is labelled in degrees or
radians to match, and \code{binwidth} is given in the same unit. A frame
declaring no angular unit is read as radians.
\item \strong{Zero and sense:} \code{0} points along \code{+x}, drawn to the right, and angles
count from \code{x} toward \code{y}. Whether that is counter-clockwise or clockwise
on the page comes from \code{\link[anicore:get_angle_direction]{anicore::get_angle_direction()}}: a frame with \code{y}
pointing up is drawn counter-clockwise, and one with \code{y} pointing down,
such as image coordinates, clockwise. A frame whose axis directions are
unknown is drawn counter-clockwise, the sense of \code{atan2()}.
\item \strong{Range:} angles are wrapped onto the circle before binning, so signed
angles in \verb{(-pi, pi]}, angles in \verb{[0, 2*pi)} and unwrapped angles all
give the same plot, and a distribution straddling \code{pi} is not split. The
axis is labelled in the range the column uses: signed when it holds
negative angles, otherwise from \code{0} to a full turn.
}

The bins are centred on multiples of the bin width, starting at \code{0}, so
with the default 36 bins of 10 degrees the four directions along the axes
each fall in the middle of a bin.

Wedges show the share of each group's angles, or with \code{weight}, the share
of its total weight. They start from an empty inner circle, \code{inner_radius}
of the way out, which keeps the narrow tips of the wedges from crowding the
centre; set it to \code{0} for a classic rose. By default a wedge's area, rather
than its length, is proportional to its share, as in \code{circular::rose.diag()}
and \code{tectonicr::rose()}: a wedge of share \code{n} reaches from the inner radius
\code{r0} out to \code{sqrt(r0^2 + k * n)}, with \code{k} set so that the largest reaches
the edge. With \code{equal_area = FALSE} its length is proportional to its share
instead, which makes peaks stand out more but exaggerates them. The radial
axis is labelled in shares either way. Missing angles and weights are left
out.

Weighting is useful for directions of travel, such as \code{course}: when an
animal is nearly still, its direction of travel is set by tracking jitter,
and those frames can swamp the ones where it is going somewhere. Weighting
by \code{speed} counts each frame by how far it moved.

With \code{show_mean = TRUE}, a thin line marks each group's circular mean
direction (\code{\link[anicore:circ_mean]{anicore::circ_mean()}}, weighted when \code{weight} is given), running
from the inner circle to the edge. It shows the direction only, not how
concentrated the angles are around it. When the angles are close to
uniform, the mean resultant length is small and the mean direction is
poorly defined, so read the line together with the wedges.

Groups are the frame's trajectory groups (\code{\link[anicore:get_keys]{anicore::get_keys()}}), coloured
with \code{\link[=palette_animovement]{palette_animovement()}} as in \code{\link[=plot_timeseries]{plot_timeseries()}}. The \code{layout}
